00:15:41.619 --> 00:15:43.033
don't do other stuff and

00:15:43.033 --> 00:15:47.705
the same thing at the end of the day 6 to
8 I just don't touch work, my laptop etc.

00:15:47.705 --> 00:15:52.470
And it's been a really good forcing
function and really well received within

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:56.071
not just my company and being but
with my clients, right?

00:15:56.071 --> 00:16:00.308
They actually do respect that we're
all human and have families and

00:16:00.308 --> 00:16:05.501
other obligations outside of work and it's
It's refreshing to be able to just have

00:16:05.501 --> 00:16:09.270
those conversations as to humans, like,
hey, here are my boundaries or limits.

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:13.302
And I've found that there have been
times when this job has been busier than

00:16:13.302 --> 00:16:14.650
other times.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:17.737
But throughout those kinds
of honest communicated with

00:16:17.737 --> 00:16:20.695
conversations have been what
have broken through and

00:16:20.695 --> 00:16:23.880
made sure that it was kind
of the best of all worlds.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:25.920
So, 10 years, and it's still going great.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:26.880
I love it.

00:16:29.252 --> 00:16:32.062
&gt;&gt; What are the great things
about consulting too?

00:16:32.062 --> 00:16:36.716
Is it that there's more ability to
control how that lifestyle might look for

00:16:36.716 --> 00:16:40.292
you than you might get in some
other industries like that?

00:16:40.292 --> 00:16:44.069
I joined BCG with a classmate at the GSB
who convinced me that we should apply to

00:16:44.069 --> 00:16:48.370
BCG together, and we've kind of tracked
each other's careers all the way through.

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:53.167
And we've taken a very different routes
in terms of what we're interested in,

00:16:53.167 --> 00:16:57.749
but then also how we balance travel or
how we think about the role of the job and

00:16:57.749 --> 00:16:58.407
our wife.

00:16:58.407 --> 00:17:01.531
So she's married,
expecting her first child, and

00:17:01.531 --> 00:17:05.190
has a dog and optimizes for,
she doesn't want to travel.

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:06.966
She's also, she's a extrovert.

00:17:06.966 --> 00:17:08.920
So she loves being in the office.

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:10.540
She loves those big group settings.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:12.950
She's been able to create that setting for
herself.

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:18.960
I don't live with anyone,
don't have a dog, and I'm an introvert.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:23.488
So I'm able to arrange my day around the
kind of things that give me energy, which

00:17:23.488 --> 00:17:28.460
plenty of alone time, to think through a
problem and then talk to people about it.

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:30.710
I also wanted to maximize the travel.

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:33.190
So, I lived out of a suitcase for
two years, and

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:37.310
I bounced around the world doing projects,
and it was a great adventure.

00:17:37.310 --> 00:17:40.010
And it sounded like the worst
thing in the world to her.

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.311
So I think one of the great things,
they were at the same firm,

00:17:42.311 --> 00:17:44.060
started in the same office.

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:48.854
So I think what's great is that you have
the ability to really control how you want

00:17:48.854 --> 00:17:50.490
the career to look for you.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:53.221
&gt;&gt; And I'll just add, Scott,

00:17:53.221 --> 00:17:56.190
that's totally true over
the course of the career too.

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:58.510
In my first years in consulting,
I wanted the same thing.

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:00.040
I wanted to be traveling all the time.

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:01.720
I went and did six months in Paris.

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.403
I did the whole global thing.

00:18:03.403 --> 00:18:05.656
And then, when I want to change, and

00:18:05.656 --> 00:18:09.670
the great thing about my job was
that it was able to change with me.

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:11.770
And that was sort of
an unexpected benefit.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:15.235
I don't know but I was like solving for
that when I was at the GSB applying for

00:18:15.235 --> 00:18:16.120
consulting jobs.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:17.608
It's been great.

00:18:20.047 --> 00:18:24.330
&gt;&gt; All right, maybe switching gears to
talk a little bit about recruiting.

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:28.073
So, some of you may have gone
through the recruiting process for

00:18:28.073 --> 00:18:33.010
consulting jobs relatively recently as you
were finishing up your GSB experience.

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:36.840
Others of you might actually be involved
in the recruiting process now and

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:41.930
helping to interview and screen resumes
and things within your respective firms.

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:46.123
How could you shed some light on what
the recruiting process within consulting

00:18:46.123 --> 00:18:46.830
looks like?

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:50.259
How people might best prepare for
that or come up to speed and

00:18:50.259 --> 00:18:54.179
be successful in those interviews and
in that recruiting process?

00:19:01.729 --> 00:19:06.080
&gt;&gt; I think Rachel was hinting at this when
she was talking about the benefits of

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:07.030
the GSB.

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:10.023
As much as you can do in
terms of practicing cases,

00:19:10.023 --> 00:19:14.100
so each firm has a little bit of
a different style of how we do cases.

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:20.190
But overall, there's a case study
involved across all of our firms.

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:24.930
So, taking the time to really practice
with a peer, so not only yourself trying

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:29.410
to do it and kind of self reflect,
I think that is really, really helpful.

00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:33.634
My only small tip would be also like don't
overdo it to a point where it sounds

00:19:33.634 --> 00:19:38.056
scripted because there's also a little
bit of a tipping point that is reached at

00:19:38.056 --> 00:19:38.790
some point.

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:43.668
But I don't know, Rachel,
do you have other other tips as well?

00:19:43.668 --> 00:19:47.260
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no,
I mean that's the right advice.

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:52.202
One thing just to start with that's
really nice about the GSB is that they

00:19:52.202 --> 00:19:57.650
actually protect your first,
I think it's maybe two months at school.

00:19:57.650 --> 00:20:01.760
So, it's really nice because it allows you
to get acquainted with being a student.

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:04.860
Again, and you really don't
have to think about recruiting.

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:06.661
And then once that does begin,

00:20:06.661 --> 00:20:11.305
I think it's great to talk to your fellow
classmates who have done consulting,

00:20:11.305 --> 00:20:15.395
figure out what they really liked
about the job, how that matches what

00:20:15.395 --> 00:20:19.719
your career ambitions are, and
then spend time doing a lot of case craft.

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:24.860
But honestly, that's the crux of
the interview is you won't be doing cases.

00:20:24.860 --> 00:20:28.760
And so, it gets really important to
figure out the structure of that.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:32.750
But as Andrea said, don't overdo it,
don't become too robotic.

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:36.650
Always approach it as if you were
actually in the job yourself day one.

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:40.650
What are the big questions
that you're trying to answer?

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:41.620
And then go from there.

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:49.860
&gt;&gt; All right, we'll turn to maybe some
questions from our audience here.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:54.523
And so we have one that says, for someone
who's looking to pivot into consulting and

00:20:54.523 --> 00:20:58.397
maybe doesn't have as much day to
day insight, could you let us know,

00:20:58.397 --> 00:21:01.222
what's the part of your role
that you both enjoy and

00:21:01.222 --> 00:21:04.789
what is the part of your role in
consulting that you least enjoy?

00:21:13.975 --> 00:21:16.190
&gt;&gt; I can start us off.

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:19.796
And I think as the panel has
already started to highlight,

00:21:19.796 --> 00:21:23.476
the consulting journey for
each person is so individual and

00:21:23.476 --> 00:21:25.990
you can tweak different dynamics of it.

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:28.920
So, I imagine my answers will
be very different than others.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:34.121
The thing that I like the most
about consulting that excites

00:21:34.121 --> 00:21:39.218
me each day is working with my team,
the Elisa Deloitte and

00:21:39.218 --> 00:21:42.770
I believe so in other consulting firms.

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:44.492
We're very team oriented,

00:21:44.492 --> 00:21:48.663
and we work on a problem together
bringing different perspectives.

00:21:48.663 --> 00:21:53.368
And that approach, I feel has allowed
me to continue to grow every day.

00:21:53.368 --> 00:21:56.590
I'm learning something
new from my teammates.

00:21:56.590 --> 00:22:02.591
It's also, as I've moved up in consulting,
then you play more of a manager role, and

00:22:02.591 --> 00:22:08.020
that also comes with its own stretching
of how do I make the best environment for

00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:10.012
the team members around me?

00:22:10.012 --> 00:22:14.684
And that, I think, is a really fun,
challenging, energizing problem and

00:22:14.684 --> 00:22:16.876
to constantly be working through.

00:22:16.876 --> 00:22:19.620
So I think that's my highlight.

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:24.771
And maybe we do highlights around, or
one other person just highlights before we

00:22:24.771 --> 00:22:27.432
go to what's more challenging in the role

00:22:34.569 --> 00:22:38.266
&gt;&gt; I love the fact that I get to dig
into problems that excite me, and

00:22:38.266 --> 00:22:41.912
I know what excites me is going to
be different from someone else.

00:22:41.912 --> 00:22:43.810
But I, just like at the GSB, right?

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:47.792
I love the fact that I got to take
whatever classes I wanted in my second

00:22:47.792 --> 00:22:51.860
year and including across these
three courses is same in consulting.

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:55.880
I get to dig into how do state
governments set their budgets?.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:57.520
And that's the thing that
really gets me excited.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:00.172
Everybody else may think it's a snoozer,
but

00:23:00.172 --> 00:23:03.930
everybody gets to kind of find
the thing that excites them.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:07.274
I really, I like the fact that
the problems are, there's stuff that I

00:23:07.274 --> 00:23:10.579
care about and stuff that feels like
it matters, and I got to pick it.

00:23:13.749 --> 00:23:16.354
&gt;&gt; Any other?
&gt;&gt; Relationships that I've been building

00:23:16.354 --> 00:23:19.523
not just, so I get to have my
Bain family here, my teams and

00:23:19.523 --> 00:23:23.363
my colleagues I've gotten to know
over time and worked with closely.

00:23:23.363 --> 00:23:27.422
But then, I also have my clients who at
this point I've got asked somebody asked

00:23:27.422 --> 00:23:30.692
in the Q and A like your clients,
was like yeah, they're mine,

00:23:30.692 --> 00:23:34.172
they're people that I've built
really deep relationships with.

00:23:34.172 --> 00:23:38.230
And I know their families, I know their
aspirations, I give them career advice.

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:40.260
It's a very deep relationship.

00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:44.583
And I love that about consulting that
I've had the opportunity to build those

00:23:44.583 --> 00:23:45.206
out over time.

00:23:47.338 --> 00:23:49.446
&gt;&gt; Anything on the other side of the coin?

00:23:49.446 --> 00:23:51.418
Rachel, sorry, you're going to chime in?

00:23:51.418 --> 00:23:54.070
&gt;&gt; I mean, it's similar to
what other folks have said.

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:57.946
But the problems that we're facing
are problems that are challenging enough

00:23:57.946 --> 00:24:01.887
that in others parts are like, okay,
we need someone else to come in and help.

00:24:01.887 --> 00:24:04.720
And to me, that's really motivating,
and really exciting.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:09.260
It's also you're really focused
on cracking this one problem.

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:13.297
I remember when I was working in CPG,
before business school, I felt like,,

00:24:13.297 --> 00:24:15.253
there were a ton of things on my plate,

00:24:15.253 --> 00:24:18.210
my attention constantly got
drawn to different places.

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:20.468
And it didn't allow me to like, really,

00:24:20.468 --> 00:24:24.789
like focus on sort of some of the biggest
issues that I really wanted to tackle and

00:24:24.789 --> 00:24:28.036
I love that I get to be really
dedicated to that in this role.

00:24:28.036 --> 00:24:32.020
And like everyone said to I really like
how we have this case team environment

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:34.820
where everyone's really in it together.

00:24:34.820 --> 00:24:38.360
And the collaboration with the client
is really inspiring as well.

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:41.894
So there's a lot of elements in
this job that are really fantastic.

00:24:45.344 --> 00:24:47.898
&gt;&gt; Jonathan,
did you have anything you wanted to add or

00:24:47.898 --> 00:24:49.530
do you want to go to the dark side?

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:52.620
&gt;&gt; I think it's been sad but
I think the people element as well,

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:56.619
like many of the same folks that you'd
be going to business school with develop

00:24:56.619 --> 00:24:59.920
great relationships will be
on your consulting teams.

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:03.857
Or there'll be the ones that are your
peers when you're in the office on Fridays

00:25:03.857 --> 00:25:05.520
or during COVID maybe every day.

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:09.794
But when you're in the office on Fridays,
they'll be the ones that you're hanging

00:25:09.794 --> 00:25:13.600
out with and it's not something that
he'll probably see in any other job.

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:18.403
&gt;&gt; How about some of the downsides or some
of the things that you're least excited

00:25:18.403 --> 00:25:20.200
about within the consulting industry?

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:22.300
Just to be fair and
look at the other side of the coin.

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:29.555
&gt;&gt; Since I started as a photographer, I
can start on the other side too, which for

00:25:29.555 --> 00:25:34.511
me, I think, as I am thinking about
consulting versus what's next,

00:25:34.511 --> 00:25:37.802
one of the things I've
felt in consulting is.

00:25:37.802 --> 00:25:40.798
We had, as Richard was talking like,

00:25:40.798 --> 00:25:44.637
dedicated on one core
problem all together, but

00:25:44.637 --> 00:25:50.174
then we I understandably hand it off for
an implementation to happen.

00:25:50.174 --> 00:25:55.070
Or for further thinking to happen or for
it to be embedded in your organization.

00:25:55.070 --> 00:26:01.120
But I often then don't see
the reach the customer side of it.

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:05.900
So if we're thinking upstream,
I have a lot of focus there.

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:10.640
I'm wondering for me, the next question
I'm doing with my own career is.

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:12.290
What does that look like downstream?

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:18.770
How do I like that the implementation part
the owning something start to finish.

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:22.274
We're owning start to finish
the answer to some problems, but

00:26:22.274 --> 00:26:25.580
thinking about the full
life-cycle of an organization and

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:29.240
thinking about the organizational
beyond that, and so on.

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:33.084
A lot of the classes of the GSB have
exposed me to that level of thinking that

00:26:33.084 --> 00:26:36.410
I'm now curious about,
which I have less insight into.

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:42.147
And that would be, I think one of my
challenges with consulting for right now.

00:26:42.147 --> 00:26:45.681
&gt;&gt; I think the other thing they were
all like tiptoeing around like,

00:26:45.681 --> 00:26:46.715
it's long hours.

00:26:46.715 --> 00:26:48.540
It's part of the industry,

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:52.845
I think across the sector is just
how consulting has been set up.

00:26:52.845 --> 00:26:55.485
It has been improving and
hopefully it keeps on improving.

00:26:56.595 --> 00:27:01.096
But it is a demanding job because of
what everybody has said of we're solving

00:27:01.096 --> 00:27:05.755
problems that were we brought in because
somebody else couldn't solve them.

00:27:05.755 --> 00:27:11.534
So therefore, it's usually a big task.

00:27:13.918 --> 00:27:14.946
&gt;&gt; I will say though,

00:27:14.946 --> 00:27:18.640
I know I'm at a point where I think
my clients work harder than I do.

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:21.820
So it just there's a time
out there where [LAUGH] and

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:26.741
I definitely get to take sabbaticals and
time off because of the project based

00:27:26.741 --> 00:27:31.830
work a little bit more easily than I think
some of my clients who are in industry.

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:36.780
So there's a shining light out there and
I would also say just.

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:40.100
There is a period of time where people are
like okay, so you're doing consulting for

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:42.760
now but
like What's your real career going to be?

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:47.108
And I would just say like as Scott and
Ryan another subset like lots of

00:27:47.108 --> 00:27:51.458
different ways that you enter and
sometimes exit consulting but I,

00:27:51.458 --> 00:27:55.750
have times I've had to say like,
no, I really enjoy consulting.

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:58.592
This is my career like, and
there's an identity there and

00:27:58.592 --> 00:28:01.550
a path there and there's a lot of joy and
fulfillment there.

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:05.250
So, sometimes that's a big question that
people have that you end up feeling a lot.

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:11.360
&gt;&gt; All right, we have another
question here from our audience,

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:15.472
given the location of Stanford right
there in the hardest Silicon Valley and

00:28:15.472 --> 00:28:19.283
this hotbed of innovation and
all sorts of different things going on.

00:28:19.283 --> 00:28:24.112
What swayed you to consulting or helped
you to stay the course in consulting if

00:28:24.112 --> 00:28:28.050
you are already in it,
versus maybe moving to private equity or

00:28:28.050 --> 00:28:32.080
venture capital or
technology or something else?

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:35.020
How did the location kind
of impact your experience?

00:28:35.020 --> 00:28:37.766
And maybe make you even more
sad about consulting, or

00:28:37.766 --> 00:28:40.340
expose you to different
things that help you now?

00:28:47.557 --> 00:28:51.884
&gt;&gt; I think it was, and this also
vary depending on class by class,

00:28:51.884 --> 00:28:55.387
every class is a little
bit of a different.

00:28:55.387 --> 00:28:58.457
My personal experience
with like Stanford and

00:28:58.457 --> 00:29:03.029
consulting was, it wasn't,
especially in during the first year.

00:29:03.029 --> 00:29:08.461
It was not the highest, let's say,
ranked preference that I would say like,

00:29:08.461 --> 00:29:11.360
what did you do before going to Stanford?

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:13.330
And my answer would be I was in McKinsey.

00:29:13.330 --> 00:29:16.007
And the answer was usually like kind of,

00:29:16.007 --> 00:29:21.047
because people are much more excited about
the I started and I did a startup or

00:29:21.047 --> 00:29:26.810
I was doing this like weird thing in
the middle of nowhere and stuff like that.

00:29:26.810 --> 00:29:30.036
And that was much more energizing and
attention grabbing,

00:29:30.036 --> 00:29:32.750
especially in the first couple months.

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:36.390
Once we started getting closer to summer
internships that quickly shifted.

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:43.570
But to the implied question of like,
was it easy to stay the course?

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:44.200
For me?

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:48.439
It wasn't like I definitely felt a little
bit more the pressure of expanding and

00:29:48.439 --> 00:29:51.930
like looking again and
rethinking everything what is out there.

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:58.466
In the end, I didn't find something that
was as exciting to me as continuing down

00:29:58.466 --> 00:30:03.548
the consulting path like to what Casey was
saying of like that being my career so

00:30:03.548 --> 00:30:05.740
I decided to stay.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:10.292
But it was definitely the signaling
around it is definitely on like expand,

00:30:10.292 --> 00:30:13.575
explore, search, like do new and
different things.

00:30:13.575 --> 00:30:17.745
So there was a little bit
of going against that.

00:30:22.005 --> 00:30:26.369
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I did not come to the GSB
thinking I was going to be a consultant

00:30:26.369 --> 00:30:31.269
when I left And even probably right up
till about a month before graduation,

00:30:31.269 --> 00:30:34.636
I didn't think I would be
a consultant when I left and

00:30:34.636 --> 00:30:36.805
try it a lot of different things.

00:30:36.805 --> 00:30:40.613
I think Stanford's location meant that I
had the ability to try a lot of different

00:30:40.613 --> 00:30:42.240
things and to try them seriously.

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:45.060
So I work for a venture fund.

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:48.310
We founded a social enterprise in Nepal.

00:30:48.310 --> 00:30:51.827
I've worked for local government,
I tried a lot of different things,

00:30:51.827 --> 00:30:53.599
in addition to trying consulting.

00:30:53.599 --> 00:30:56.678
And I think that the location
of the Stanford network gave me

00:30:56.678 --> 00:30:59.509
the data that I needed to be able to say,
okay, I've,

00:30:59.509 --> 00:31:02.050
I thought all these things
could be interested.

00:31:02.050 --> 00:31:05.557
I tried all of them now I have the
information I need to know that consulting

00:31:05.557 --> 00:31:08.161
was the right thing for me,
but it certainly wasn't,

00:31:08.161 --> 00:31:10.160
didn't didn't come with that mission.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:13.840
But Stanford is a place that
encourages experimentation I loved.

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:17.712
I don't know if it's the same approach but
a lot of the career counseling process,

00:31:17.712 --> 00:31:21.330
they're very much said, No, don't go
reconfirm things you already know.

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:24.169
Try things you don't know
about to get the information

00:31:24.169 --> 00:31:27.080
about what you do you don't like and
what works for you.

00:31:27.080 --> 00:31:30.880
And I'm really glad I
ended up in consulting and

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:35.725
also really glad that Silicon Valley
meant that it was really

00:31:35.725 --> 00:31:39.531
easy to try a bunch of
other things in parallel.

00:31:39.531 --> 00:31:40.850
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:42.684
&gt;&gt; Just to add to that, too, about Sam for

00:31:42.684 --> 00:31:45.700
providing the opportunities to
test waters and a lot of spaces.

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:47.000
I think that's absolutely true.

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:51.675
And because of its location, you get kind
of CEOs of high tech companies coming in

00:31:51.675 --> 00:31:56.078
to talk to you and also coursework if
anyone's interested in creating their

00:31:56.078 --> 00:31:58.301
own company, their startup garage.

00:31:58.301 --> 00:32:01.014
And I had a couple of friends
go through startup garage, and

00:32:01.014 --> 00:32:04.323
most of them realize, what, I don't
think I'm going to be a founder and

00:32:04.323 --> 00:32:07.180
went on to find jobs at other companies.

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:11.072
For me, one thing that was,
I don't know kind of won me over for

00:32:11.072 --> 00:32:14.166
consulting is kind of what
Ryan alluded into too.

00:32:14.166 --> 00:32:19.240
Is you can start in consulting, but still
pivot into another industry afterwards.

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:22.889
I really feel there isn't really a
downside of starting in consulting unless

00:32:22.889 --> 00:32:25.761
you know exactly what you want to do and
it's not consulting.

00:32:25.761 --> 00:32:29.380
It gives you a really good opportunity
to explore and grow a lot.

00:32:29.380 --> 00:32:34.378
And the skills that you'll gain
from consulting similar to Stanford

00:32:34.378 --> 00:32:39.203
will help you in whatever career that
you want to do afterwards if you

00:32:39.203 --> 00:32:43.786
decide eventually you do want to
be industry or role focused so.

00:32:43.786 --> 00:32:45.726
&gt;&gt; All right,
we have another question here.

00:32:45.726 --> 00:32:50.199
Are there any must take courses or
experiences that really helped you build

00:32:50.199 --> 00:32:54.034
your strategic thinking or
leadership skills for consulting?

00:32:56.820 --> 00:32:58.191
&gt;&gt; I can take that on Lisa.

00:32:58.191 --> 00:33:02.360
I don't think that there were any
in particular that were must take.

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:06.674
So I would say that there's two that
are just general GSB must takes that I

00:33:06.674 --> 00:33:07.816
would recommend.

00:33:07.816 --> 00:33:11.190
So touchy feely, which is also
known as interpersonal dynamics,

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:13.070
which is a great class will help you.

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:17.178
One just generally in all
of your relationships but

00:33:17.178 --> 00:33:21.332
probably also help you relate to
your clients as well and consulting.

00:33:21.332 --> 00:33:25.665
And then the other one that I found to be
incredibly helpful was managing growing

00:33:25.665 --> 00:33:27.994
enterprises which just helped you have and

00:33:27.994 --> 00:33:31.499
roleplay a lot of difficult
conversations that you might have.

00:33:31.499 --> 00:33:36.103
With clients or with some folks on your
team as well, certainly other classes,

00:33:36.103 --> 00:33:37.556
maybe that are helpful.

00:33:37.556 --> 00:33:40.853
But, I think for all of the firms
that are represented here,

00:33:40.853 --> 00:33:44.940
there's a fair amount of on boarding and
training in terms of hard skills.

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:48.797
So I'd probably focus a bit
more on the interpersonal

00:33:48.797 --> 00:33:51.461
soft stuff while you're at the GSB.

00:33:51.461 --> 00:33:55.897
&gt;&gt; I just have to say I'm relieved to hear
that the two classes I was going to say

00:33:55.897 --> 00:34:00.191
are still being told by graduating
recently that makes me feel warm and

00:34:00.191 --> 00:34:01.526
fuzzy and not so old.

00:34:01.526 --> 00:34:02.501
So that's very cool.

00:34:02.501 --> 00:34:07.061
[LAUGH] Two thumbs up for
MGE and interpersonal dynamics.

00:34:07.061 --> 00:34:12.378
And then I would just emphasize that part
about, because I saw a few questions

00:34:12.378 --> 00:34:18.149
in the chat about non-profit backgrounds
or less traditional backgrounds.

00:34:18.149 --> 00:34:21.976
I think all the firms are focused on
making sure that the hard skills you need

00:34:21.976 --> 00:34:25.740
like how to do a piano or how to use
Excel, you're going to get those skills on

00:34:25.740 --> 00:34:28.495
the job, the training is excellent and
consulting.

00:34:28.495 --> 00:34:34.072
So the time of the GSB is precious for
a lot of more GM oriented and

00:34:34.072 --> 00:34:37.556
touchy feely types of classes I think.

00:34:37.556 --> 00:34:44.482
&gt;&gt; The other classes or experiences
that you draw upon today, anyone else?

00:34:44.482 --> 00:34:49.070
&gt;&gt; As in general I'd just say take
the risk when you're at the GSB.

00:34:49.070 --> 00:34:51.068
So because of great non disclosure and

00:34:51.068 --> 00:34:55.389
because of the fact that the student body
is incredibly supportive of each other.

00:34:55.389 --> 00:34:59.534
Take the harder version of the course if
you're on the fence between the base level

00:34:59.534 --> 00:35:01.346
and the harder level of marketing.

00:35:01.346 --> 00:35:02.813
Take the advanced course or

00:35:02.813 --> 00:35:05.889
you're not certain about that
across the street court.

00:35:05.889 --> 00:35:07.507
Take the risk.

00:35:07.507 --> 00:35:09.910
It's an incredibly safe environment.

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:14.900
I think that the times when I pushed
myself into those more advanced courses or

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:15.666
I took the,

00:35:15.666 --> 00:35:20.835
international policy course across the
street that I had no business being in.

00:35:20.835 --> 00:35:23.908
Were the ones that weren't where
I actually learned the most

00:35:28.818 --> 00:35:30.143
&gt;&gt; And the favorites?

00:35:31.793 --> 00:35:32.852
&gt;&gt; I can build on that too.

00:35:32.852 --> 00:35:35.954
I mean, I think one thing that's
great about Stanford is yes,

00:35:35.954 --> 00:35:39.288
you do have classes that are set up
with the typical case structure,

00:35:39.288 --> 00:35:41.800
but you have a lot of
different formats too.

00:35:41.800 --> 00:35:44.579
And some of the other
formats is what I love a lot.

00:35:44.579 --> 00:35:47.421
I took a class my second year
called leadership perspective,

00:35:47.421 --> 00:35:51.097
where we just brought in really inspiring
leaders from different industries.

00:35:51.097 --> 00:35:52.949
To tell us about their career journey and

00:35:52.949 --> 00:35:56.120
how they thought about their
leadership style over the course of it.

00:35:56.120 --> 00:36:00.374
And at the end, I had the opportunity to
sit down reflect what kind of leader do I

00:36:00.374 --> 00:36:03.019
want to be tomorrow in five
years in ten years, and

00:36:03.019 --> 00:36:06.160
to me that's a really great opportunity.

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:11.003
I also kind of like MGE as Jonathan said,
there's a class called managing it typical

00:36:11.003 --> 00:36:13.919
conversations and
it's all about role playing.

00:36:13.919 --> 00:36:18.453
And sometimes you don't realize how much
you learn from actually just trying to do

00:36:18.453 --> 00:36:19.250
it yourself.

00:36:19.250 --> 00:36:22.865
It's different to think something
to actually try speaking it and

00:36:22.865 --> 00:36:24.810
I loved those classes at Stanford.

00:36:24.810 --> 00:36:28.920
And then lastly,
there's a lot of design oriented classes.

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:31.489
Right, I think you had
mentioned that earlier,

00:36:31.489 --> 00:36:33.567
I took a class called Equity by Design.

00:36:33.567 --> 00:36:37.704
And I've actually directly used some of
the principles I learned that class and

00:36:37.704 --> 00:36:40.063
the diversity equity
inclusion [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:40.063 --> 00:36:43.581
So, yeah,
Stanford offers very different formats, or

00:36:43.581 --> 00:36:47.850
a lot of very different formats and
they're all really, valuable.

00:36:50.390 --> 00:36:54.086
&gt;&gt; I'm curious if any of you were involved
in the consulting club during your time

00:36:54.086 --> 00:36:54.647
on campus?

00:36:54.647 --> 00:36:58.511
And if so, how that was that experience
and in addition to the consulting club,

00:36:58.511 --> 00:37:02.275
what were some other helpful resources
that helped you with the recruiting?

00:37:02.275 --> 00:37:05.250
I know you mentioned classmates and
such earlier but

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:09.317
are there any other things either
consulting club or other resources?

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:22.869
&gt;&gt; I feel the alumni network
deserves a mention as well.

00:37:22.869 --> 00:37:28.480
I wanted to move geographies as
part of recruiting for consulting.

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:32.243
So, I called up the alumni
in Australia and said,

00:37:32.243 --> 00:37:35.837
I'm interested in making
a move to Australia.

00:37:35.837 --> 00:37:38.280
Can you help me through this process?

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:40.290
I think that was absolutely invaluable.

00:37:40.290 --> 00:37:43.649
It's also great to be able to talk
to alumni from multiple firms.

00:37:43.649 --> 00:37:47.193
To be able to get a sense for
how the different firms operate.

00:37:47.193 --> 00:37:51.600
And so beyond the people that are on
campus there to help you and your peers.

00:37:51.600 --> 00:37:55.005
Know that people who are part of a broader
community I found absolutely invaluable

00:37:55.005 --> 00:37:56.102
throughout the process.

00:38:00.131 --> 00:38:02.859
&gt;&gt; Does any of you rely on
career management advisors or

00:38:02.859 --> 00:38:06.021
career management support during
your time there as you were

00:38:06.021 --> 00:38:10.241
making this transition or deciding if you
wanted to stay in consulting or not or?

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:20.522
&gt;&gt; I ended up using the CMC
as what it's called.

00:38:20.522 --> 00:38:25.901
I'll call it more of like a therapy
session around my career progression and

00:38:25.901 --> 00:38:29.780
where I wanted to go,
which was some great listeners.

00:38:31.270 --> 00:38:36.028
I've just like helping me verbalize out
loud where I wanted to track my career in

00:38:36.028 --> 00:38:38.371
the short term and in the longer term and

00:38:38.371 --> 00:38:41.301
then that meant actually for
my GSB experience.

00:38:41.301 --> 00:38:47.130
So kind of taking it full circle, I found
them helpful in thinking through that.

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:52.210
And then I also went to a deck
because we're all central

00:38:52.210 --> 00:38:57.409
on Stanford's campus and
the GSB is tucked in a corner.

00:38:57.409 --> 00:39:01.815
I used what we call NBA twos when I was
an NBA ones, when I was a first year,

00:39:01.815 --> 00:39:05.738
the second year is to kind of bounce
ideas back and forth as well.

00:39:05.738 --> 00:39:10.900
So it was, if we think of lots of
different levels of people to engage,

00:39:10.900 --> 00:39:13.230
I also found them useful.

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:17.335
You're surrounded by so many people all
thinking about those same questions.

00:39:21.049 --> 00:39:25.243
&gt;&gt; We've talked a little bit about some of
the resources that folks have use as they

00:39:25.243 --> 00:39:27.760
were thinking about
the consulting industry.

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:33.041
There's a question here if consulting
really as a focus at the GSB or

00:39:33.041 --> 00:39:38.892
not, Andre, I think you've kind of
maybe highlighted that a little bit.

00:39:38.892 --> 00:39:42.237
When you first arrived and how that
shifted over your time there as it got

00:39:42.237 --> 00:39:45.010
closer to summer internships and such.

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:48.886
But did you feel that the school
tried to either push you towards or

00:39:48.886 --> 00:39:53.630
push you away from consulting and is sort
of the gist of this question, did you?

00:39:57.501 --> 00:40:02.489
&gt;&gt; I didn't feel that the school or the
class or the teachers themselves tried to

00:40:02.489 --> 00:40:05.866
push me towards or
away from consulting or towards or

00:40:05.866 --> 00:40:11.287
away from any other career I think a lot
of the conversation was focused on,

00:40:11.287 --> 00:40:15.848
just more the concept of a leader that
I think Rachel was mentioning of,

00:40:15.848 --> 00:40:18.550
what type of leader do you want to be?

00:40:18.550 --> 00:40:22.411
Obviously, aligned with the the school's
[INAUDIBLE] as well like what type of

00:40:22.411 --> 00:40:23.820
changes do you want to drive?

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:26.859
What good do you want to
bring to the world, etc.?

00:40:26.859 --> 00:40:30.764
So that piece there was a lot of
focus on reflecting on that and

00:40:30.764 --> 00:40:33.607
learning the skills to build towards that.

00:40:33.607 --> 00:40:39.545
But I don't think at any point
there was a kind of industry poach.

00:40:39.545 --> 00:40:41.106
Some of the classes,

00:40:41.106 --> 00:40:46.136
I feel like do assume a little bit
of a private sector more like focus,

00:40:46.136 --> 00:40:52.407
but there's also where I leaned on more of
like social innovation and all that track.

00:40:52.407 --> 00:40:56.273
And a lot of the classes were like,
okay, if you were the CEO of this,

00:40:56.273 --> 00:40:57.352
what would you do?

00:40:57.352 --> 00:41:01.479
So, there is an assumption
of that type of career, but

00:41:01.479 --> 00:41:06.257
not in a specific industry,
would be my personal experience.

00:41:09.765 --> 00:41:11.880
&gt;&gt; All right, I see a question here.

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:15.618
Obviously, there are a lot
of different wonderful

00:41:15.618 --> 00:41:18.427
consulting firms out there you work for
many of them.

00:41:18.427 --> 00:41:23.756
There are also different types of
consulting even within various firms, too.

00:41:23.756 --> 00:41:26.765
How did you find the right fit for
you, both the company and

00:41:26.765 --> 00:41:29.729
the type of work that you're
doing within that company.

00:41:29.729 --> 00:41:34.487
And are there different personality
types by firm or capabilities that firms

00:41:34.487 --> 00:41:39.480
are looking for, tell me a little bit more
about that fit and finding your place?

00:41:49.208 --> 00:41:53.871
&gt;&gt; I'll take a stab at this final
round sir, interesting one.

00:41:53.871 --> 00:41:58.458
So, I think once upon a time we were
all recruiting for consulting, and

00:41:58.458 --> 00:42:02.128
we're all kind of randomly
picking which ones we like and

00:42:02.128 --> 00:42:05.347
we've all got our own
different personalities.

00:42:05.347 --> 00:42:08.794
And I don't know that any of
the firms are necessarily looking for

00:42:08.794 --> 00:42:12.556
different personalities, but
I do think the firms will have different

00:42:12.556 --> 00:42:15.570
personalities that
resonate better with you.

00:42:15.570 --> 00:42:19.084
And I think that during
the recruiting process,

00:42:19.084 --> 00:42:22.350
you will find just what works better for
you?

00:42:22.350 --> 00:42:27.280
That could both be the firm's actual
culture or it could also be who in

00:42:27.280 --> 00:42:32.760
particular from the firm shows up for
that particular cultivation event.

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:35.669
So it's a little bit of
learning on your part, but

00:42:35.669 --> 00:42:40.610
I certainly know that McKinsey is not out
there looking for a specific personality.

00:42:40.610 --> 00:42:44.546
And, in fact, we're actually
looking to have be as inclusive and

00:42:44.546 --> 00:42:47.780
work for as many different
types of folks as possible so

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:51.225
we can get as many different
voices as we make decisions and

00:42:51.225 --> 00:42:56.009
make sure that we're representing our
clients and our clients customers as best

00:42:56.009 --> 00:43:00.056
we can in these rooms where we're
making recommendations to them.

00:43:04.897 --> 00:43:09.794
&gt;&gt; Ask just even from within your firms,
trying to figure out, do you want to be

00:43:09.794 --> 00:43:15.670
more of a generalist or have a particular
industry focus or other area of expertise?

00:43:15.670 --> 00:43:16.620
How did you navigate that?

00:43:16.620 --> 00:43:18.327
Or how much time do you have to decide?

00:43:18.327 --> 00:43:21.233
Or things like that,
that might be helpful for our audience.

00:43:26.215 --> 00:43:30.880
&gt;&gt; Again, I mean, I think on that one,
it's probably a little bit up to you.

00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:35.424
So, I think for all of our firms,
there's probably generalists, and

00:43:35.424 --> 00:43:39.592
maybe certainly McKinsey there
are folks that are expert focus so

00:43:39.592 --> 00:43:42.870
they may focus on a specific function or
industry.

00:43:42.870 --> 00:43:45.090
Or particular growth area.

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:48.427
And so,
I know that McKinsey is really excited and

00:43:48.427 --> 00:43:53.690
has this thing where you can kind of
do whatever you want to some extent.

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:56.236
They call it make your own McKinsey.

00:43:56.236 --> 00:44:01.648
But, basically, if you are most excited
about doing clean technology work or

00:44:01.648 --> 00:44:06.158
sustainability work, like I was,
you can focus on that either by

00:44:06.158 --> 00:44:10.668
being a generalist or
being exclusively an expert on that topic.

00:44:10.668 --> 00:44:15.393
And so, to some extent, it really just
comes down to what you want to do and

00:44:15.393 --> 00:44:19.352
then trying to find the right
people at McKinsey, at least.

00:44:19.352 --> 00:44:23.040
And I don't know how it works at the other
firms that are represented here.

00:44:23.040 --> 00:44:28.716
You can try and make that particular the
patch work, but you really need to have

00:44:28.716 --> 00:44:34.656
some conversations with different folks
to create some of those opportunities.

00:44:34.656 --> 00:44:37.409
&gt;&gt; I will say consultants
are really good at aliteration, so

00:44:37.409 --> 00:44:40.554
you've got Make Your Own McKinsey and
we have Build Your Own Bean,

00:44:40.554 --> 00:44:42.422
but it's the same idea in consulting.

00:44:42.422 --> 00:44:46.876
Which is, you, over time, can start to
shape it but you don't come in at least to

00:44:46.876 --> 00:44:50.625
intubate, you do not come in having
to wear one hat and not another.

00:44:50.625 --> 00:44:54.194
And you'll actually get an opportunity
to wear a lot of different hats, and

00:44:54.194 --> 00:44:56.280
see which industries
do you enjoy the most?

00:44:56.280 --> 00:44:58.363
What type of work do you
end up enjoying the most?

00:44:58.363 --> 00:45:02.420
And, by now, I probably have three or
four different hats that I interchange.

00:45:02.420 --> 00:45:04.910
I'm a healthcare expert.

00:45:04.910 --> 00:45:07.270
I'm an organizational change expert.

00:45:07.270 --> 00:45:11.428
There's a few different hats that I
can wear depending on the context, but

00:45:11.428 --> 00:45:15.795
those were built over time and because
that was what I ended up enjoying doing.

00:45:15.795 --> 00:45:19.614
But I didn't go insane that's
what I'm going to go do, so.

00:45:19.614 --> 00:45:22.914
&gt;&gt; To some extent when you start
can't know what the answer for

00:45:22.914 --> 00:45:23.976
you is going to be.

00:45:23.976 --> 00:45:27.086
I mean I came in laser focused I
wanted to do economic development,

00:45:27.086 --> 00:45:29.930
public sector consulting, and
that's what I wanted to do.

00:45:29.930 --> 00:45:34.381
And thankfully I really clicked with the
people at ECG, who do that sort of work,

00:45:34.381 --> 00:45:38.372
but I've also had a lot of my colleagues
who joined out of the GSB common,

00:45:38.372 --> 00:45:41.579
they not have any interest in retail but
really clicked and

00:45:41.579 --> 00:45:46.229
had strong relationships with that client
retail or with what our managing director

00:45:46.229 --> 00:45:50.420
in retail and they've now become
partners in the retail practice of ECG.

00:45:50.420 --> 00:45:54.078
So, it's so much of it's going to be
driven by the choice that you want to make

00:45:54.078 --> 00:45:57.396
and the stuff that excites you and
what you're passionate about.

00:45:57.396 --> 00:45:59.957
But it's also going to be about
the people that you meet and

00:45:59.957 --> 00:46:01.766
where you build those relationships.

00:46:01.766 --> 00:46:06.382
Because, as everybody said earlier on,
in the end, it's a team sport,

00:46:06.382 --> 00:46:09.657
it's a people business, and
it's going to be about,

00:46:09.657 --> 00:46:13.323
who are you going to want to
work with over the longer term.

00:46:13.323 --> 00:46:17.377
Or when we eventually get back to travel,
that cliche of who are you going to

00:46:17.377 --> 00:46:20.981
want to be stuck in an airport with
when your flight gets delayed.

00:46:20.981 --> 00:46:25.343
And that day to day experiences is really,
I think,

00:46:25.343 --> 00:46:28.129
what makes it for a lot of people.

00:46:28.129 --> 00:46:29.676
&gt;&gt; Here's a great question.

00:46:29.676 --> 00:46:30.657
Can you speak to,

00:46:30.657 --> 00:46:34.852
maybe, a moment that you knew you made
the right decision to come to the GSB?

00:46:34.852 --> 00:46:38.740
It could be a particular moment,
a class, an interaction, a lecture.

00:46:40.150 --> 00:46:42.802
Whatever it might be,
what was that moment for you?

00:46:52.461 --> 00:46:54.800
&gt;&gt; For me, something near and

00:46:54.800 --> 00:46:59.589
near to my heart from the GSB
is a program we called Talk.

00:46:59.589 --> 00:47:03.362
Where two students each
week from each class,

00:47:03.362 --> 00:47:09.071
go up in front of their classmates and
share life-defining moments,

00:47:09.071 --> 00:47:13.714
things that inspire them,
why they are the way they are,

00:47:13.714 --> 00:47:17.220
could be just the essay what matters most.

00:47:18.580 --> 00:47:23.837
And I know I gave my personal talk
on January of my first year, so

00:47:23.837 --> 00:47:29.891
we had a couple of months under balance,
but we're still settling in and

00:47:29.891 --> 00:47:35.870
that feeling afterwards is something
that will stick with me forever.

00:47:37.160 --> 00:47:44.140
The incredible support of peers even
some of them were nice and friends.

00:47:44.140 --> 00:47:46.120
Some of them were still strangers.

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:52.933
But that feeling of support, of being
held by a room of 200 of your classmates,

00:47:52.933 --> 00:47:56.886
just because you've
shared about yourself and

00:47:56.886 --> 00:48:01.824
gone to the GSB occurency and
it is around vulnerability and

00:48:01.824 --> 00:48:09.490
that's shared throughout the entire
school, throughout the way that we create.

00:48:09.490 --> 00:48:14.236
We've talked about touchy feely we
create relationships etc., that.

00:48:14.236 --> 00:48:18.965
That moment at the end of my talk was
something I wish I could bottle up.

00:48:18.965 --> 00:48:23.081
But I knew in that moment I
was in the right place for

00:48:23.081 --> 00:48:25.342
me at the right time for me.

00:48:25.342 --> 00:48:29.291
And that is more personal than
professional but it has tied into how I

00:48:29.291 --> 00:48:33.462
create professional relationships
with clients across teams, etc.

00:48:37.649 --> 00:48:40.705
&gt;&gt; Anyone else wanted to share a moment
when they knew they'd made the right

00:48:40.705 --> 00:48:41.214
decision?

00:48:43.741 --> 00:48:45.929
&gt;&gt; I can go.

00:48:45.929 --> 00:48:50.240
I think I knew it from the moment I
stepped on campus on my first day.

00:48:51.310 --> 00:48:53.498
And there were so many moments
during the course of the tears.

00:48:53.498 --> 00:48:56.914
We'll share a personal line that's a
recent, so it's not the first moment, but

00:48:56.914 --> 00:48:57.917
it's a recent moment.

00:48:57.917 --> 00:49:00.478
Is I'm planning my wedding and
as you do that,

00:49:00.478 --> 00:49:03.910
you think about who you're
going to invite right?

00:49:03.910 --> 00:49:08.464
And a majority of my best friends in
life are now the people who I've met at

00:49:08.464 --> 00:49:09.580
Stanford.

00:49:09.580 --> 00:49:13.055
And I cannot tell you how much,
like they mean to me.

00:49:13.055 --> 00:49:16.884
And, it doesn't always happen but
the people at Stanford

00:49:16.884 --> 00:49:21.116
are really like the best people I
personally have met in my life.

00:49:21.116 --> 00:49:25.297
And, I can't imagine not
having the experience now so.

00:49:29.859 --> 00:49:32.925
&gt;&gt; Any other moments,
classes, interactions?

00:49:37.008 --> 00:49:40.329
&gt;&gt; Another one I can
throw out there as well.

00:49:40.329 --> 00:49:43.565
I agree like I would second talk,
of course,

00:49:43.565 --> 00:49:48.337
others would be throughout the journey
in the Arbuckle leadership

00:49:48.337 --> 00:49:52.955
program that I think Jonathan
maybe had mentioned that as well.

00:49:52.955 --> 00:49:55.390
And just overall echoing the talk piece,

00:49:55.390 --> 00:49:58.950
I think Stanford is just very
good at dealing with emotions.

00:49:58.950 --> 00:50:04.001
In a way they asked me to
participate in a roleplay in front

00:50:04.001 --> 00:50:10.560
of classmates that we were being
trained to be coaches basically.

00:50:10.560 --> 00:50:13.296
And during that roleplay
I was a coachee and

00:50:13.296 --> 00:50:17.552
they started nudging me into like,
topics of identity and race and

00:50:17.552 --> 00:50:21.673
a ton of like things and
under like crying in front of everybody.

00:50:21.673 --> 00:50:26.130
And the fact that it was fine
like it was not a surprise.

00:50:26.130 --> 00:50:31.095
It was almost like in whatever I
mentioned of like the room kind

00:50:31.095 --> 00:50:33.204
of like holds you in a way.

00:50:33.204 --> 00:50:36.173
I think that was like my moment of.

00:50:36.173 --> 00:50:39.931
I kind of fit here,
this is a good place to be right now.

00:50:42.550 --> 00:50:46.294
&gt;&gt; We, tragically lost three
classmates from my class at Stanford.

00:50:46.294 --> 00:50:50.345
And I hesitate to share that
because it's really sad moment.

00:50:50.345 --> 00:50:53.789
But honestly the way
the community came around,

00:50:53.789 --> 00:50:59.256
it just instantly bonded us like it
happened in the first few weeks of school.

00:50:59.256 --> 00:51:03.420
And so, that sticks out to me because
there was that moment where we all looked

00:51:03.420 --> 00:51:07.518
at each other and we were like, we're
going to help each other get through this

00:51:07.518 --> 00:51:10.555
incredibly difficult time and
from people in my class.

00:51:10.555 --> 00:51:14.585
And also the way the school handled it was
like an immediate moment of like, wow,

00:51:14.585 --> 00:51:16.139
I'm in the exact right place.

00:51:16.139 --> 00:51:20.500
So grateful to this day for how the school
handled that and the people around me.

00:51:23.775 --> 00:51:27.749
&gt;&gt; A lot of very moving examples.

